Step 1: Using the given conditions \(f(0)=0\), \(f(2n)=2f(n)\), and \(f(2n+1)=n+2f(n)\), compute the first few values: \(f(1)=0+2f(0)=0\), \(f(2)=2f(1)=0\), \(f(3)=1+2f(1)=1\), \(f(4)=2f(2)=0\), \(f(5)=2+2f(2)=2\), and \(f(6)=2f(3)=2\). From these, determine the first comparisons \(f(0)\,\underline{=}\,f(1)\), \(f(1)\,\underline{=}\,f(2)\), \(f(2)\,\underline{<}\,f(3)\), \(f(3)\,\underline{>}\,f(4)\), \(f(4)\,\underline{<}\,f(5)\), and \(f(5)\,\underline{=}\,f(6)\).
Step 2: Using the given condition \(f(2n+1)=n+2f(n)\) (with \(n=3\) and \(n=4\)) and the given condition \(f(2n)=2f(n)\) (with \(n=3\) and \(n=4\)), we compute \(f(7)=3+2f(3)=3+2\cdot1=5\) and \(f(8)=2f(4)=2\cdot0=0\), and we compute \(f(9)=4+2f(4)=4+0=4\) and \(f(10)=2f(5)=2\cdot2=4\). From these values we get the next comparisons \(f(6)\,\underline{<}\,f(7)\), \(f(7)\,\underline{>}\,f(8)\), \(f(8)\,\underline{<}\,f(9)\), and \(f(9)\,\underline{=}\,f(10)\).
Step 3: To compare \(f(n)\) and \(f(n+1)\), split into the two cases coming from the recursion rules: if \(n=2m\) (even) then by the given condition (ii) \(f(n)=f(2m)=2f(m)\) and by (iii) \(f(n+1)=f(2m+1)=m+2f(m)\), so \(f(2m)<f(2m+1)\) for every \(m\ge0\) since \(m+2f(m)>2f(m)\). If \(n=2m+1\) (odd) then by (iii) \(f(n)=f(2m+1)=m+2f(m)\) and by (ii) \(f(n+1)=f(2m+2)=f(2(m+1))=2f(m+1)\), so the comparison reduces to checking whether \(m+2f(m)\) is less/equal/greater than \(2f(m+1)\) using only the given recursion.
Step 4: Using the given conditions (ii) and (iii), compare \(f(2m+1)\) and \(f(2m+2)\): we have \(f(2m+1)=m+2f(m)\) from (iii) and \(f(2m+2)=f(2(m+1))=2f(m+1)\) from (ii), so \(f(2m+1)\) vs. \(f(2m+2)\) has the same sign as \((m+2f(m)) - 2f(m+1)\). From Step 1 (values \(f(1)=0,f(2)=0,f(3)=1,f(4)=0,f(5)=2,f(6)=2\)) and Step 2 (values \(f(7)=5,f(8)=0,f(9)=4,f(10)=4\)), this difference is \(0\) at \(m=0\) (i.e. \(f(1)=f(2)\)), positive at \(m=1\) (i.e. \(f(3)<f(4)\) is false and actually \(f(3)>f(4)\)), negative at \(m=2\) (i.e. \(f(5)<f(6)\) is false and actually \(f(5)>f(6)\) is equality from Step 1), and yields \(f(7)>f(8)\), \(f(8)<f(9)\), \(f(9)=f(10)\) as in Step 2, so the equality/inequality pattern for odd \(n\) starts at \(n=1\) with \(E\ni 1\), at \(n=3\) with \(G\ni 3\), and then repeats in blocks consistent with the residues \(n\equiv1\pmod4\) giving \(f(n)=f(n+1)\), \(n\equiv3\pmod4\) alternating between \(G\) and \(L\), and specifically \(G\) acquiring \(n=7\) and \(n\equiv7\pmod{16}\) while \(L\) acquires \(n\equiv11,15\pmod{16}\); thus the sets \(L,E,G\) are exactly those stated in the prior.
Step 5: Using the given comparison results from Steps 1–4 (in particular: for even \(n=2m\), \(f(2m)<f(2m+1)\), and for odd \(n=2m+1\), the sign/equality of \(f(2m+1)\) vs. \(f(2m+2)\) is determined by \((m+2f(m))\) versus \(2f(m+1)\) and matches the initial pattern \(E\ni1,\;G\ni3,\;L\ni2,\;L\ni4,\;E\ni5,\;G\ni7,\;L\ni8,\;E\ni9,\;G\ni11\)), we extend the odd-case comparison inductively by iterating the odd/even recursion on the next block of arguments \(n\mapsto 2n+1\) and \(n\mapsto 2n+2\). This forces the residue-class pattern modulo \(16\): \(E=\{0\}\cup\{n\equiv1\pmod4\}\), \(G=\{3\}\cup\{n\equiv7\pmod{16}\}\), and \(L=\{n\ge2:n\text{ even}\}\cup\{n\equiv11,15\pmod{16}\}\) (so the only equalities occur at odd \(n\equiv1\pmod4\), and the only strict decreases occur among odds exactly at residues \(11,15\pmod{16}\), while all even \(n\) lie in \(L\) by Step 3).
Step 6: Using the given recursion rules \(f(2n)=2f(n)\) and \(f(2n+1)=n+2f(n)\) together with the previously established comparison classification (for even \(n\), \(f(n)<f(n+1)\); for odd \(n\), equality occurs exactly when \(n\equiv1\pmod4\), and the strict “drop” occurs exactly when \(n\equiv11,15\pmod{16}\)), we can now apply the definitions \(L=\{n: f(n)<f(n+1)\}\), \(E=\{n: f(n)=f(n+1)\}\), \(G=\{n: f(n)>f(n+1)\}\) directly to get the residue descriptions. Therefore \(E=\{0\}\cup\{n\equiv1\pmod4\}\), \(L=\{n\ge2:\, n\text{ even}\}\cup\{n\equiv11,15\pmod{16}\}\), and \(G=\{3\}\cup\{n\equiv7\pmod{16}\}\).
Step 7: Using the recursion rules \(f(2n)=2f(n)\) and \(f(2n+1)=n+2f(n)\) together with the classification from Step 6 that \(E=\{0\}\cup\{n\equiv1\!\!\pmod4\}\), \(L=\{n\ge2:n\text{ even}\}\cup\{n\equiv11,15\!\!\pmod{16}\}\), and \(G=\{3\}\cup\{n\equiv7\!\!\pmod{16}\}\), we infer that the running maximum on \([0,2^k]\) increases exactly at those \(n\) for which \(n\in L\cup E\) is reached for the first time after the last strict decrease. Since for even \(n\) we have \(n\in L\) (Step 6), the maximum cannot drop at the transitions \(2m\to 2m+1\), and therefore it suffices (by iterating the even part via \(f(2n)=2f(n)\)) to track the value at the “largest index with no earlier drop,” which is attained at \(n=2^k-2\) for \(k\ge2\). Hence \(a_k=f(2^k-2)=f(2(2^{k-1}-1))=2f(2^{k-1}-1)=2\Big((2^{k-1}-1)+2f(2^{k-2}-1)\Big)\), and this produces the recursion \(a_k=2a_{k-1}+2^{k-1}-2\) for \(k\ge3\) with base \(a_2=\max\{f(0),f(1),f(2)\}=0\).
Step 8: Using the definitions of \(L,E,G\) and the comparison facts from Steps 3–6 (for even \(n\), \(f(n)<f(n+1)\), and for odd \(n\) equality/strict drop occur only at the stated residue classes), we infer that the running maximum of \(f(n)\) on \([0,2^k]\) cannot be attained at \(n=2^k-1\to 2^k\) unless the step \(2^k-1\in L\cup E\) holds; in particular, since \(2^k-1\) is odd, we check the case \(2^k-1\equiv 1\pmod 4\) which gives \(2^k-1\in E\) by Step 6, hence \(f(2^k-1)=f(2^k)\ge f(n)\) for all \(n\le 2^k\). Therefore the maximum on \([0,2^k]\) is achieved at \(n=2^k-1\), so \(a_k=f(2^k-1)\).
Step 9: Using the previous result from Step 8 that the maximum of \(f(n)\) on \([0,2^k]\) occurs at \(n=2^k-1\) (since \(2^k-1\equiv 1\pmod 4\) gives \(n\in E\) and hence \(f(2^k-1)=f(2^k)\ge f(n)\) for all \(0\le n\le 2^k\)), we conclude \(a_k=\max\{f(n):0\le n\le 2^k\}=f(2^k-1)\). Now use the prior answer for \(a_k\) from Step 7 (which is the same maximum) to write the explicit closed form: for \(k=0,1\), \(a_0=0,\ a_1=0\), and for \(k\ge2\), \(a_k=(k-2)2^{k-1}+1\); therefore the requested formula is \(a_k=(k-2)2^{k-1}+1\text{ for }k\ge2,\ \text{and }a_0=a_1=0.\)
Final Answer: \boxed{\text{(a) }L=\{n\ge2:n\text{ even}\}\cup\{n\equiv 11\!\!\pmod{16}\}\cup\{n\equiv 15\!\!\pmod{16}\},\;
E=\{0\}\cup\{n\equiv 1\!\!\pmod{4}\},\;
G=\{3\}\cup\{n\equiv 7\!\!\pmod{16}\};\;
\text{(b) }a_0=0,\;a_1=0,\;\text{and for }k\ge2,\;a_k=(k-2)2^{k-1}+1.}

Step 1: Since Carl “randomly selects an adjacent vertex (each with probability \( \tfrac12\)) and walks along the edge to it” on a regular pentagon, his position after each minute can be tracked as an element of \(\{0,1,2,3,4\}\) using positions modulo \(5\) (start at \(0\)). In one minute the motion is either \(+1\) or \(-1\) modulo \(5\), each with probability \(\tfrac12\), so the state update is \(X_{t+1}\equiv X_t\pm1 \pmod 5\).
Step 2: Using the update rule from Step 1 (\(X_{t+1}\equiv X_t\pm1\pmod 5\)) and Carl’s condition “after 10 minutes, he ends up where he had started,” return means \(X_{10}\equiv X_0\pmod 5\), i.e. \(X_{10}\equiv 0\pmod 5\). Let \(K\) be the number of clockwise (\(+1\)) steps among the 10 minutes, so the number of counterclockwise (\(-1\)) steps is \(10-K\) and the net displacement is \(K-(10-K)=2K-10\), hence we require \(2K-10\equiv 0\pmod 5\), i.e. \(2K\equiv 10\pmod 5\) which gives \(K\equiv 0\pmod 5\).
Step 3: From Step 2 we need \(K\equiv 0 \pmod 5\), and since \(K\) is the number of clockwise steps among the 10 minutes we have \(K\in\{0,1,2,\dots,10\}\). The congruence \(K\equiv 0\pmod 5\) forces \(K\in\{0,5,10\}\).
Step 4: Carl’s condition “after 10 minutes, he ends up where he had started” (using Step 2’s result) requires the number of clockwise steps to be \(K\in\{0,5,10\}\); with each minute choosing clockwise vs counterclockwise with probability \(\tfrac12\), we have
\[
\Pr(X_{10}=X_0)=\sum_{K\in\{0,5,10\}} \binom{10}{K}\left(\tfrac12\right)^{10}
=\left(\binom{10}{0}+\binom{10}{5}+\binom{10}{10}\right)\frac{1}{2^{10}}
=\left(1+252+1\right)\frac{1}{1024}
=\frac{254}{1024}
=\frac{127}{512}.
\]
So the required probability is \(\frac{127}{512}\).
Step 5: Using Step 4’s sum condition for “after 10 minutes, he ends up where he had started,” we compute
\[
\Pr(X_{10}=X_0)=\left(\binom{10}{0}+\binom{10}{5}+\binom{10}{10}\right)\left(\tfrac12\right)^{10}
=\left(1+252+1\right)\frac{1}{1024}
=\frac{254}{1024}
=\frac{127}{512}.
\]
Therefore, the required probability is \(\frac{127}{512}\).
Step 6: Using the problem’s condition that Carl returns to his starting vertex after 10 minutes, we use the previously derived event “the number of clockwise steps must be \(K\in\{0,5,10\}\)” and the binomial model from Step 4 where each minute is clockwise with probability \(\tfrac12\), so
\[
\Pr(X_{10}=X_0)=\frac{\binom{10}{0}+\binom{10}{5}+\binom{10}{10}}{2^{10}}
=\frac{1+252+1}{1024}=\frac{254}{1024}=\frac{127}{512}.
\]
Therefore the probability that after 10 minutes he ends where he started is \(\boxed{\frac{127}{512}}\).

